WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.472
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:05.877 --> 00:00:07.710
MAX SALTONSTALL: My
name is Max Saltonstall.

00:00:07.710 --> 00:00:10.950
And I am in need of a new couch.

00:00:10.950 --> 00:00:13.622
But finding a new couch
is not actually that easy,

00:00:13.622 --> 00:00:15.330
especially when I
don't really want to go

00:00:15.330 --> 00:00:16.920
into a whole bunch of stores.

00:00:16.920 --> 00:00:21.060
If only there is some way
that I could virtually

00:00:21.060 --> 00:00:24.550
see a whole lot of
different couch options.

00:00:24.550 --> 00:00:30.390
Hm, I know a guy who works
at a company that I think

00:00:30.390 --> 00:00:31.510
can help us.

00:00:31.510 --> 00:00:32.910
I'm here with
Barry from Marxent.

00:00:32.910 --> 00:00:34.300
Barry, thanks for
joining me today.

00:00:34.300 --> 00:00:36.795
Can you tell me a little bit
about what you and Marxent do?

00:00:36.795 --> 00:00:37.920
BARRY BESECKER: Hi, Max.

00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:41.220
I'm the CTO and
Cofounder of Marxent.

00:00:41.220 --> 00:00:44.400
And we provide content
management systems

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:48.000
for 3D products as
well as applications

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:51.720
for ecommerce experiences
for retailers and brands,

00:00:51.720 --> 00:00:53.350
specifically products
for the home.

00:00:53.350 --> 00:00:55.017
MAX SALTONSTALL: So
finding a new couch,

00:00:55.017 --> 00:00:58.272
because I need a new couch, how
can Marxent help me do that?

00:00:58.272 --> 00:00:59.730
BARRY BESECKER: So
Marxent provides

00:00:59.730 --> 00:01:04.170
room-planning applications and
augmented reality applications

00:01:04.170 --> 00:01:09.570
and web AR experiences that let
consumers come in and design

00:01:09.570 --> 00:01:12.270
and layout a new floor
plan for their kitchen,

00:01:12.270 --> 00:01:14.670
for their bedroom, dining
room, or living room.

00:01:14.670 --> 00:01:16.680
And they take 3D
products that are

00:01:16.680 --> 00:01:20.220
the actual products that
they can buy from a retailer

00:01:20.220 --> 00:01:21.660
or from a manufacturer.

00:01:21.660 --> 00:01:23.760
And they can arrange those
products in the space.

00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:25.320
They can try out
different styles.

00:01:25.320 --> 00:01:28.200
They can switch their wall
paint, their flooring,

00:01:28.200 --> 00:01:29.190
and their lighting.

00:01:29.190 --> 00:01:32.583
And they can really design
their future living space.

00:01:32.583 --> 00:01:35.250
And then when they're done, they
can add all those items to cart

00:01:35.250 --> 00:01:38.140
and check out on a retailer
or a manufacturer's website.

00:01:38.140 --> 00:01:41.820
So that's what we do is bring
those room planning and AR

00:01:41.820 --> 00:01:43.195
experiences to
life for the home.

00:01:43.195 --> 00:01:44.528
MAX SALTONSTALL: That's awesome.

00:01:44.528 --> 00:01:46.830
So that's going to be really
helpful when I can't just

00:01:46.830 --> 00:01:48.497
go to a whole bunch
of furniture stores,

00:01:48.497 --> 00:01:50.370
but I want to know
what my options are.

00:01:50.370 --> 00:01:53.940
Can you walk me through
what is the life of, say,

00:01:53.940 --> 00:01:57.660
one couch as it goes from
an idea in some furniture

00:01:57.660 --> 00:02:01.235
designer's mind to eventually
landing in my living room?

00:02:01.235 --> 00:02:01.985
Where do we start?

00:02:01.985 --> 00:02:04.830
BARRY BESECKER: So it all starts
with a content order, where

00:02:04.830 --> 00:02:07.140
we'll work with a
client who, let's say,

00:02:07.140 --> 00:02:12.780
wants to put 3,000 couches with
different fabrics, leathers,

00:02:12.780 --> 00:02:16.500
different wood
finishes, whatever

00:02:16.500 --> 00:02:19.950
it happens to be into their
room-planning application.

00:02:19.950 --> 00:02:21.630
So they'll work
with us to identify

00:02:21.630 --> 00:02:24.150
what those SKUs are going
to be and provide us

00:02:24.150 --> 00:02:25.110
reference materials.

00:02:25.110 --> 00:02:28.650
Sometimes that's a CAD
engineering drawings.

00:02:28.650 --> 00:02:32.460
Sometimes it's high-poly
assets that they've used

00:02:32.460 --> 00:02:34.830
for a photography replacement.

00:02:34.830 --> 00:02:36.510
Sometimes it's just photos.

00:02:36.510 --> 00:02:40.050
And sometimes it's just a link
to their PDP on their website.

00:02:40.050 --> 00:02:41.100
We'll take those assets.

00:02:41.100 --> 00:02:43.440
And then we'll go through
a planning exercise.

00:02:43.440 --> 00:02:45.330
Now, a planning
exercise will identify

00:02:45.330 --> 00:02:46.950
with a unique set
of assets that we

00:02:46.950 --> 00:02:50.880
need to create to
accomplish that order.

00:02:50.880 --> 00:02:52.560
One of the things
about the home space

00:02:52.560 --> 00:02:54.690
is a lot of the
products in a room can

00:02:54.690 --> 00:02:59.460
share similar materials, can
share similar subcomponents.

00:02:59.460 --> 00:03:02.790
For instance, if you think about
a sofa, you're buying the sofa.

00:03:02.790 --> 00:03:03.930
You have the cushions.

00:03:03.930 --> 00:03:05.970
You have the feet on the sofa.

00:03:05.970 --> 00:03:08.370
You have pillows
that may be shared

00:03:08.370 --> 00:03:11.880
across the other items in
the room, like a love seat,

00:03:11.880 --> 00:03:14.640
for instance, that's styled
in the same collection.

00:03:14.640 --> 00:03:17.310
So we'll identify
even subcomponents

00:03:17.310 --> 00:03:21.120
of those products that can
be built once or designed

00:03:21.120 --> 00:03:23.760
once and then reused
multiple times,

00:03:23.760 --> 00:03:25.140
which really brings
down the cost

00:03:25.140 --> 00:03:28.530
and effort around maintenance
or when changes come in

00:03:28.530 --> 00:03:30.610
for that order.

00:03:30.610 --> 00:03:34.050
So once that planning
exercise is complete,

00:03:34.050 --> 00:03:35.790
we'll go through
a routing exercise

00:03:35.790 --> 00:03:37.575
through our CMS application.

00:03:37.575 --> 00:03:38.700
MAX SALTONSTALL: Very cool.

00:03:38.700 --> 00:03:42.090
So in the very
beginning, retailer says,

00:03:42.090 --> 00:03:43.500
here's my 3,000 couches.

00:03:43.500 --> 00:03:46.380
Luckily, I only need one couch,
but you might need 3,000.

00:03:46.380 --> 00:03:47.490
Where does that come to?

00:03:47.490 --> 00:03:50.040
What does it sit in
when you absorb that?

00:03:50.040 --> 00:03:52.110
BARRY BESECKER: So the
back end of our CMS

00:03:52.110 --> 00:03:57.690
is a custom relational database
that runs on Cloud SQL.

00:03:57.690 --> 00:04:01.620
And then for the file assets,
textures, images, the reference

00:04:01.620 --> 00:04:03.870
material, 3D meshes--

00:04:03.870 --> 00:04:09.280
all of those are stored
in Cloud Storage.

00:04:09.280 --> 00:04:11.520
And so the custom
application is front

00:04:11.520 --> 00:04:15.090
ended by an Angular application
that runs on Firebase.

00:04:15.090 --> 00:04:22.810
So all the artists, the
planners, the analytics,

00:04:22.810 --> 00:04:28.270
all of those users use that
3D workflow application

00:04:28.270 --> 00:04:31.660
to manage the entire process,
really, from beginning to end.

00:04:31.660 --> 00:04:35.652
And then once it's published,
those assets end up on a CDN.

00:04:35.652 --> 00:04:36.610
MAX SALTONSTALL: Great.

00:04:36.610 --> 00:04:38.770
So assets come in.

00:04:38.770 --> 00:04:41.740
Artists might be making
new textures or new models.

00:04:41.740 --> 00:04:43.490
They're all being
listed in the database,

00:04:43.490 --> 00:04:46.640
and that's keeping track of
how they relate to each other.

00:04:46.640 --> 00:04:49.240
This is a type of wood, or
this is a type of linen.

00:04:49.240 --> 00:04:52.300
And then at some point, they
need to be combined, right?

00:04:52.300 --> 00:04:54.990
You need to know that my
couch needs to be Naugahyde

00:04:54.990 --> 00:04:56.240
because that's very important.

00:04:56.240 --> 00:05:00.370
So how are you doing that
combination ahead of time?

00:05:00.370 --> 00:05:03.490
Because I'm assuming you don't
want to do it right on demand

00:05:03.490 --> 00:05:05.710
when I'm trying to put that
couch in my living room.

00:05:05.710 --> 00:05:06.640
BARRY BESECKER: Absolutely.

00:05:06.640 --> 00:05:08.020
And that's really one
of the big lessons

00:05:08.020 --> 00:05:09.610
we learned early
on as our session

00:05:09.610 --> 00:05:13.030
started to scale that
this relational database

00:05:13.030 --> 00:05:15.220
system that we
built for building

00:05:15.220 --> 00:05:18.190
these individual assets
is great for management,

00:05:18.190 --> 00:05:21.370
great for maintenance,
great for reusability,

00:05:21.370 --> 00:05:24.970
and really reduces the effort
around that content creation

00:05:24.970 --> 00:05:25.863
lifecycle.

00:05:25.863 --> 00:05:27.280
When it comes to
consumption, it's

00:05:27.280 --> 00:05:30.880
really not a great environment
to pull all those assets

00:05:30.880 --> 00:05:31.720
on demand.

00:05:31.720 --> 00:05:34.210
We even found that
in many cases,

00:05:34.210 --> 00:05:36.460
pulling an individual product
because of all the reuse

00:05:36.460 --> 00:05:40.000
could end up with 50 different
queries that need to be run,

00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:44.080
which could really put a large
load on a content management

00:05:44.080 --> 00:05:44.960
system.

00:05:44.960 --> 00:05:51.250
So we developed is a
compile-time set of features

00:05:51.250 --> 00:05:53.800
that would notice whenever
an artist would change

00:05:53.800 --> 00:05:56.530
one individual
component of a product,

00:05:56.530 --> 00:05:59.080
notifying all the products
that would be affected

00:05:59.080 --> 00:06:00.910
by that individual change.

00:06:00.910 --> 00:06:03.340
And then really,
at compile time,

00:06:03.340 --> 00:06:08.980
just like you have nightly job
services for a DevOps team,

00:06:08.980 --> 00:06:10.735
would compile and
basically build

00:06:10.735 --> 00:06:13.660
a runtime version of that
product that can then

00:06:13.660 --> 00:06:15.820
be published to CDN at scale.

00:06:15.820 --> 00:06:19.330
What's nice about that is then
when the end application wants

00:06:19.330 --> 00:06:23.333
to consume that 3D product, it
just downloads that manifest

00:06:23.333 --> 00:06:25.000
and then pulls all
the individual assets

00:06:25.000 --> 00:06:27.940
that needs to render that
without really ever affecting

00:06:27.940 --> 00:06:30.025
the core content
management operation.

00:06:30.025 --> 00:06:31.150
MAX SALTONSTALL: It's cool.

00:06:31.150 --> 00:06:33.160
So does that mean
that you're generating

00:06:33.160 --> 00:06:38.230
new assets, new composite assets
in the Cloud Storage buckets,

00:06:38.230 --> 00:06:41.830
tracking the relationship
between the core pieces

00:06:41.830 --> 00:06:44.260
and the composites
in your database,

00:06:44.260 --> 00:06:47.810
and then publishing the
finished composites to the CDN

00:06:47.810 --> 00:06:49.420
so that they can be
easily pulled down

00:06:49.420 --> 00:06:50.672
to my laptop or my phone?

00:06:50.672 --> 00:06:51.880
BARRY BESECKER: That's right.

00:06:51.880 --> 00:06:55.480
And we've seen big benefits
with that approach.

00:06:55.480 --> 00:06:59.080
Really, our servers or our
applications never go down.

00:06:59.080 --> 00:07:01.330
You're taking advantage
of a lot of cached data

00:07:01.330 --> 00:07:03.250
that is deployed to CDN.

00:07:03.250 --> 00:07:07.030
We even take some of the
indexes around product lists

00:07:07.030 --> 00:07:10.240
and the configuration
of our applications

00:07:10.240 --> 00:07:12.940
to deploy those via
SQLite so that those

00:07:12.940 --> 00:07:16.240
can be downloaded as
little mini databases

00:07:16.240 --> 00:07:17.773
when the application starts up.

00:07:17.773 --> 00:07:19.690
So we can reduce the
chattiness back and forth

00:07:19.690 --> 00:07:22.030
through web services
as the user is

00:07:22.030 --> 00:07:23.500
experiencing the application.

00:07:23.500 --> 00:07:25.180
So really, in many
of these cases,

00:07:25.180 --> 00:07:27.280
once they start
the application up,

00:07:27.280 --> 00:07:32.110
until they're ready to
save their end room layout,

00:07:32.110 --> 00:07:34.453
they could run in a
completely offline way.

00:07:34.453 --> 00:07:36.370
And so a lot of the
information can be cached,

00:07:36.370 --> 00:07:38.770
and we wait until changes
happen to redownload it.

00:07:38.770 --> 00:07:41.110
So users can come in and
out of the application

00:07:41.110 --> 00:07:43.120
if new content has
been published.

00:07:43.120 --> 00:07:45.495
They're running pretty much
locally in the browser cache.

00:07:45.495 --> 00:07:47.078
MAX SALTONSTALL:
That sounds like it's

00:07:47.078 --> 00:07:49.900
going to make performance for
me, the consumer, a lot better

00:07:49.900 --> 00:07:52.000
but also reduce
the amount that you

00:07:52.000 --> 00:07:53.305
have to send over the network.

00:07:53.305 --> 00:07:54.430
BARRY BESECKER: Absolutely.

00:07:54.430 --> 00:07:56.050
And that's really
what it's about

00:07:56.050 --> 00:07:59.650
is kind of pleasing
these two different users

00:07:59.650 --> 00:08:00.910
of this platform.

00:08:00.910 --> 00:08:05.650
It's around letting the artists
produce the most amount of 3D

00:08:05.650 --> 00:08:07.720
products with the
least number of assets

00:08:07.720 --> 00:08:09.580
and then be able to
respond to changes that

00:08:09.580 --> 00:08:13.990
come in from the client or from
the project team as they're

00:08:13.990 --> 00:08:15.700
deploying a new application.

00:08:15.700 --> 00:08:19.780
But at the same time,
pleasing the end consumer,

00:08:19.780 --> 00:08:21.490
creating as fast
as downloads as we

00:08:21.490 --> 00:08:24.160
can with a minimum
number of assets.

00:08:24.160 --> 00:08:26.890
And so we can create the best
quality rendering experience

00:08:26.890 --> 00:08:30.460
on the front end for the amount
of assets that we're using.

00:08:30.460 --> 00:08:32.817
MAX SALTONSTALL: And in
retail, if you're too slow,

00:08:32.817 --> 00:08:34.150
someone is going to wander away.

00:08:34.150 --> 00:08:35.830
But also if your
systems go down,

00:08:35.830 --> 00:08:39.860
that downtime is lost money for
all of your retailer clients.

00:08:39.860 --> 00:08:42.280
So how do you make
sure that your back end

00:08:42.280 --> 00:08:46.332
is reliable enough to keep them
still selling all the time?

00:08:46.332 --> 00:08:48.790
BARRY BESECKER: It's really
about isolating that production

00:08:48.790 --> 00:08:52.630
environment and consumption
environment from the creation

00:08:52.630 --> 00:08:55.360
environment where all
the assets are built.

00:08:55.360 --> 00:08:57.790
And by keeping those
separate, we don't really have

00:08:57.790 --> 00:08:59.770
to worry about downtime at all.

00:08:59.770 --> 00:09:01.510
When new content
batches go out, they

00:09:01.510 --> 00:09:05.560
can be tested through, really,
an A/B testing deployment

00:09:05.560 --> 00:09:07.390
that we've implemented.

00:09:07.390 --> 00:09:10.318
And so we never really
worry about downtime

00:09:10.318 --> 00:09:11.860
when it comes to
consuming the assets

00:09:11.860 --> 00:09:13.338
or using the end applications.

00:09:13.338 --> 00:09:14.380
MAX SALTONSTALL: Awesome.

00:09:14.380 --> 00:09:17.050
It sounds like a pretty
sophisticated system

00:09:17.050 --> 00:09:19.030
that's mostly invisible
to me is I just

00:09:19.030 --> 00:09:22.180
get to enjoy virtual couches all
over, filling my living room up

00:09:22.180 --> 00:09:22.870
to the ceiling.

00:09:22.870 --> 00:09:23.500
BARRY BESECKER: That's the hope.

00:09:23.500 --> 00:09:25.930
It's been about 10 years in
building this environment

00:09:25.930 --> 00:09:28.305
and kind of solving all the
different problems that we've

00:09:28.305 --> 00:09:30.640
seen along the way with
making the process more

00:09:30.640 --> 00:09:32.950
efficient for creation
and then also making

00:09:32.950 --> 00:09:35.158
the process highly optimized
for consumption as well.

00:09:35.158 --> 00:09:36.492
MAX SALTONSTALL: That's awesome.

00:09:36.492 --> 00:09:38.660
Thank you, Barry, for
coming on and for telling us

00:09:38.660 --> 00:09:42.460
this story about how these
virtual sofas can travel

00:09:42.460 --> 00:09:44.620
through that entire
Cloud infrastructure

00:09:44.620 --> 00:09:46.600
to end up in my
virtual and hopefully,

00:09:46.600 --> 00:09:48.100
eventually, my
physical living room.

00:09:48.100 --> 00:09:49.180
I really appreciate it.

00:09:49.180 --> 00:09:49.720
BARRY BESECKER: Thanks, Max.

00:09:49.720 --> 00:09:50.450
It's been great.

00:09:50.450 --> 00:09:51.325
MAX SALTONSTALL: Wow.

00:09:51.325 --> 00:09:55.120
So now I get how all those
virtual couches ended up all

00:09:55.120 --> 00:09:56.500
over my living room.

00:09:56.500 --> 00:09:59.170
Oh, I guess I have
to pick one now, huh?

00:09:59.170 --> 00:10:01.360
Well, I'm really
glad that Barry was

00:10:01.360 --> 00:10:03.400
able to explain how
Marxent is using

00:10:03.400 --> 00:10:05.350
all these different
pieces of Google Cloud

00:10:05.350 --> 00:10:08.978
to power this super cool
retail experience from home.

00:10:08.978 --> 00:10:11.020
If you want to learn more
about what they've done

00:10:11.020 --> 00:10:13.057
and what many others have
done on Google Cloud,

00:10:13.057 --> 00:10:15.640
click on the link below to see
the rest of "Architecting Cloud

00:10:15.640 --> 00:10:16.390
Solutions."

00:10:16.390 --> 00:10:18.330
Thanks for watching.

